NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.050
In addition to L2 regularization and dropout regularization,

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:07.850
there are a few other techniques for reducing overfitting in your neural network.

00:00:07.850 --> 00:00:08.970
Let's take a look.

00:00:08.970 --> 00:00:11.370
Let's say you're fitting a CAD classifier.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.850
If you're overfitting, getting more training data can help,

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:20.410
but getting more training data can be expensive and you sometimes just can't get more data.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:24.890
But what you can do is augment your training set by taking an image like this and,

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:29.490
for example, flipping it horizontally and adding that also to your training set.

00:00:29.530 --> 00:00:32.970
Now, instead of just this one example in your training set,

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.650
you can add this to your training example.

00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:38.210
By flipping your images horizontally,

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.570
you could maybe double the size of your training set.

00:00:40.570 --> 00:00:42.930
Because your training set is now a bit redundant,

00:00:42.930 --> 00:00:47.410
this isn't as good as if you had collected an additional M set

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:50.050
of brand new independent examples,

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:56.890
but you could do this without needing to pay the expense of going out to take more pictures of CADs.

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.850
And then, other than flipping horizontally,

00:00:59.850 --> 00:01:02.210
you can also take random crops of the image.

00:01:02.210 --> 00:01:07.610
So here we're rotated and so they randomly zoom into the image and this still looks like a CAD.

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:11.130
But so by taking random distortions and transformations of the image,

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:16.450
you could augment your data set and make additional fake training examples.

00:01:16.450 --> 00:01:18.690
Again, these extra fake training examples,

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:25.850
they don't add as much information as they were to go out and get a brand new independent example of a CAD.

00:01:25.850 --> 00:01:28.290
But because you could do this, you're almost free,

00:01:28.290 --> 00:01:30.570
other than for some computational cost,

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:36.250
this can be an inexpensive way to give your data.

00:01:36.250 --> 00:01:39.050
This can be an inexpensive way to give your algorithm more data,

00:01:39.050 --> 00:01:43.050
and therefore, you know, sort of regularize it and reduce overfitting.

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:45.050
And by synthesizing examples like this,

00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:48.490
what you're really telling your algorithm is that if something is a CAD,

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.610
then flipping it horizontally is still a CAD.

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:55.730
Notice I didn't flip it vertically because maybe we don't want upside down CADs.

00:01:55.930 --> 00:02:00.210
And then also maybe randomly zooming into how the image is probably still a CAD.

00:02:00.210 --> 00:02:02.050
For optical character recognition,

00:02:02.050 --> 00:02:08.530
you can also augment your data set by taking digits and imposing random rotations and distortions to it.

00:02:08.530 --> 00:02:14.690
So if you add these things to your training set, you know, these are also still digit 4s.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:18.370
For illustration, I applied a very strong distortion.

00:02:18.370 --> 00:02:21.170
So this looks very wavy for in practice,

00:02:21.170 --> 00:02:23.930
you don't need to distort the 4 quite as aggressively,

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:28.970
but just a more subtle distortion than what I'm showing here to make this example clearer for you.

00:02:28.970 --> 00:02:32.810
Right. But the more subtle distortion is usually used in practice,

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:35.370
because this looks like really warped force.

00:02:35.370 --> 00:02:40.250
So data augmentation can be used as a regularization technique,

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.930
because it has effects similar to regularization.

00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:46.850
There's one other technique that is often used called early stopping.

00:02:46.850 --> 00:02:50.090
So what you're going to do is as you run gradient descent,

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:57.730
you're going to plot your either the trading error, your 01 classification error on the trading set,

00:02:57.730 --> 00:03:00.570
or just plot the cost function j you're optimizing,

00:03:00.570 --> 00:03:03.890
and that should decrease monotonically, like so.

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:08.970
Because as you train, hopefully your trading error or your cost function j should decrease.

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:16.850
So with early stopping, what you do is you plot this and you'd also plot your depth set error.

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:20.050
And again, this could be a classification error or your development set,

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:24.410
or something like the cost function, like the log loss,

00:03:24.410 --> 00:03:26.330
evaluated in our depth set.

00:03:26.330 --> 00:03:30.330
And what you find is that your depth set error will usually go down for a while,

00:03:30.330 --> 00:03:32.610
and then it will increase from there.

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:36.050
So what early stopping does is you would say,

00:03:36.050 --> 00:03:40.050
well, it looks like your neural network was doing best around that iteration.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:42.930
So we're just going to stop training your neural network halfway,

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:47.210
and you'll take whatever value achieved this depth set error.

00:03:47.210 --> 00:03:48.250
So why does this work?

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:52.850
Well, when you haven't run many iterations for your neural network yet,

00:03:52.850 --> 00:03:57.490
your parameters w will be close to zero because, you know, with random initialization,

00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:00.130
you probably initialize w to small random values.

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:04.090
So before you train for a long time, w is still quite small.

00:04:04.090 --> 00:04:07.690
And as you iterate, as you train, w would get bigger and bigger and bigger.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:14.010
And so here, maybe you have a much larger value of the parameters w for your neural network.

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:17.650
So what early stopping does is by stopping halfway,

00:04:17.650 --> 00:04:24.010
you have only a, you know, mid-size, right? w.

00:04:24.010 --> 00:04:26.730
And so similar to L2 regularization,

00:04:26.730 --> 00:04:30.770
by picking a neural network with smaller norm for your parameters w,

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:34.490
hopefully your neural network is overfitting less.

00:04:34.490 --> 00:04:40.650
And the term early stopping refers to the fact that you're just stopping the training of your neural network early.

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:45.490
I sometimes use early stopping when training a neural network, but it does have one downside.

00:04:45.490 --> 00:04:46.530
Let me explain.

00:04:46.530 --> 00:04:50.690
I think of the machine learning process as comprising several different steps.

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:55.530
One is that you want an algorithm to optimize the cost function j,

00:04:55.530 --> 00:05:00.170
and we have various tools to do that, you know, such as gradient descent.

00:05:00.170 --> 00:05:05.130
And then we'll talk later about other algorithms like momentum and alve-

00:05:05.130 --> 00:05:07.850
an RMS prop and Adam and so on.

00:05:07.850 --> 00:05:14.890
But then after optimizing the cost function j, you also wanted to not overfit.

00:05:14.890 --> 00:05:21.930
And we have some tools to do that, such as your regularization, getting more data, and so on.

00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:26.050
Now in machine learning, we already have so many hyperparameters to search over.

00:05:26.050 --> 00:05:31.010
It's already very complicated to choose among the space of possible algorithms.

00:05:31.010 --> 00:05:36.250
And so I find machine learning easier to think about when you have one set of tools

00:05:36.250 --> 00:05:41.050
for optimizing the cost function j, and when you're focusing on optimizing the cost function j,

00:05:41.050 --> 00:05:46.770
all you care about is finding w and b so that j of w and b is as small as possible,

00:05:46.770 --> 00:05:49.890
and you just don't think about anything else other than reducing this.

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:55.490
And then it's completely separate tasks to not overfit.

00:05:55.490 --> 00:05:57.570
In other words, to reduce variance.

00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:01.050
And when you're doing that, you have a separate set of tools for doing it.

00:06:01.050 --> 00:06:06.250
And this principle is sometimes called orthogonalization.

00:06:06.250 --> 00:06:10.810
And this is an idea that you want to be able to think about one task at a time.

00:06:10.810 --> 00:06:14.450
I'll say more about orthogonalization in a later video.

00:06:14.450 --> 00:06:17.210
So if you don't fully get the concept yet, don't worry about it.

00:06:17.210 --> 00:06:23.610
But to me, the main downside of early stopping is that this couples these two tasks.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:28.090
So you no longer can work on these two problems independently.

00:06:28.090 --> 00:06:32.850
Because by stopping gradient descent early, you're sort of breaking whatever you're doing

00:06:32.850 --> 00:06:35.970
to optimize the cost function j, because now you're not doing a great job

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:39.290
reducing the cost function j, you've sort of not done that that well.

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:43.450
And then you're also simultaneously trying to not overfit.

00:06:43.450 --> 00:06:46.330
So instead of using different tools to solve the two problems,

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:49.490
you're using one two that kind of mixes the two.

00:06:49.490 --> 00:06:56.610
And this just makes the set of things you could try more complicated to think about.

00:06:56.610 --> 00:07:01.730
Rather than using early stopping, one alternative is just use L2 regularization.

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:04.930
Then you can just train the neural network as long as possible.

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:08.930
I find that this makes the search space of hyperparameters easier to decompose

00:07:08.930 --> 00:07:10.650
and easier to search over.

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:13.690
But the downside of this, though, is that you might have to try a lot of values

00:07:13.690 --> 00:07:16.330
of the regularization parameter lambda.

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:21.970
And so this makes searching over many values of lambda more computationally expensive.

00:07:21.970 --> 00:07:27.450
And the real advantage of early stopping is that running the gradient descent process just once,

00:07:27.450 --> 00:07:31.570
you get to try out values of small w, mid-size w, and large w

00:07:31.570 --> 00:07:38.770
without needing to try a lot of values of the regularization, L2 regularization hyperparameter lambda.

00:07:40.210 --> 00:07:43.810
If this concept doesn't completely make sense to you yet, don't worry about it.

00:07:43.810 --> 00:07:47.410
When we talk about ortho-organization in greater detail in a later video,

00:07:47.410 --> 00:07:49.730
I think this will make a bit more sense.

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:52.730
Despite its disadvantages, many people do use it.

00:07:52.730 --> 00:07:57.570
I personally prefer to just use L2 regularization and try different values of lambda.

00:07:57.570 --> 00:08:00.410
That's assuming you can afford the computation to do so.

00:08:00.410 --> 00:08:04.410
But early stopping does let you get a similar effect without needing to explicitly

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:06.850
try lots of different values of lambda.

00:08:06.850 --> 00:08:12.090
So you've now seen how to use data augmentation, as well as, if you wish,

00:08:12.090 --> 00:08:17.410
early stopping in order to reduce variance and prevent overfitting your neural network.

00:08:17.410 --> 00:08:21.690
Next, let's talk about some techniques for setting up your optimization problem

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:23.690
to make your training go quickly.
